LOCAL1="Processor and graphics card settings"
LOCAL2="%s sets the processor's governor and energy preference while it runs, so the power profile leaves the processor to it."
LOCAL3="%s sets the power level of the graphics card %s, so the power profile leaves that card to it."
LOCAL4="%s and %s both set the power level of the graphics card %s, and the one that writes last wins. Keep that card's settings in one of them."
LOCAL5="%s failed (status %s), so the power profile service could not be told which settings CoreCtrl or LACT holds. Run the command again as administrator."
LOCAL6="tuned's profile also sets the power method of the graphics card %s, which %s sets too, and tuned cannot leave that card out. Keep that card's power settings in one of them."
LOCAL7="tuned could not leave the processor to %s: %s"
LOCAL8="LACT's settings file %s could not be read, so the graphics cards it holds are not known."
LOCAL9="The power profile service could not be set to leave alone the settings CoreCtrl or LACT holds: %s"
LOCAL10="The CoreCtrl rule made by hand at %s is replaced by the package's own rule; the old file is kept as %s."
LOCAL11="Your own CoreCtrl rule at %s stays as it is; polkit reads it before the package's rule."
LOCAL12="The CoreCtrl rule kept as %s is back at %s, as it was before the package."
LOCAL13="CoreCtrl's manual profile %s sets the processor's governor and energy preference. CoreCtrl does not tell other programs when you turn a manual profile on, so while it is on the power profile may change the processor too."
LOCAL14="CoreCtrl's manual profile %s sets the power level of the graphics card %s. CoreCtrl does not tell other programs when you turn a manual profile on, so while it is on the power profile may change that card too."
LOCAL15="It cannot be seen which program changes the power profile's graphics card setting (%s), so a change LACT makes as it starts is told from yours only by its timing."
